I have two concrete directions to propose: selective residue guesses across several moduli, and higher-rank additive divisor covers. Their main feasibility risks are different: the first must pay for separate bases and baby lists; the second must construct the cover uniformly and factor the differences reached during recovery. The older large-order-base bottleneck has also improved: a 2025 theorem reaches \(D\ge N^{1/6}\) in \(D^{1/2+o(1)}\) time.
